#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15624df2-9887-5ec3-8e91-fe7710aba543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561645} {:answer/id #uuid "31b1ab74-70b5-55c5-8ff9-594e80672f5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561714} {:answer/id #uuid "9fec3ede-dd6e-545e-8574-c51f46483d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561976} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b9c6fe-27e8-5acb-af1b-5a95bd5cdb89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561991} {:answer/id #uuid "b90271d8-7502-571d-8474-c5e46ce637f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562051}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561976} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6b92f20-ac1b-5794-9dbb-0cc54d1d0397" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7eede828-b363-5824-aef0-9e1007418c88" :question/math-academy-id "q-99476" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ 13 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}7-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 13 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=2\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2+\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=2+\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ 13 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5-\\text{i} & -2 \\\\ 13 & -5-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(5-\\text{i}){x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 13{x}_{1}+(-5-\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(5-\\text{i}){x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(5-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=2$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{{5}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{13}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562476 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6ab6e7a-d92d-5ecf-8a85-faffe97c6157" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562118}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562118} :answer-field/id #uuid "08b0a80b-6e11-58dd-a2f8-eea08773e1f7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562224} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "954a215e-6bc2-56e2-86bb-f73d149f278b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561952}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561952} :answer-field/id #uuid "28c5e5b8-30fa-5781-8559-a9e4f5d972ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b1b90563-ce29-5141-8ac6-0ac20bb77c12" :question/math-academy-id "q-216312" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4}p+3q=25 \\\\ p+\\frac{2}{5}q=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,q=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $4$ and the second equation by $5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4}p+3q=25 & |\\times 4 \\\\ p+\\frac{2}{5}q=0 & |\\times 5\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5p+12q=100 \\\\ 5p+2q=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-1,$ then we will get a $-5p$ term that can cancel with the $5p$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5p+12q=100 & |\\,\\times (-1) \\\\ 5p+2q=0 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-5p-12q=-100 \\\\ 5p+2q=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $p$-variable, and allows us to solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{-5p}-12q & =-100 \\\\ \\cancel{5p}+2q & =0 \\\\ \\hline (-12q+2q) & =(-100+0) \\\\ -10q & =-100 \\\\ q & =\\frac{100}{10} \\\\ q & =10\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $q=10$ into the second equation to solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5p+2q & =0 \\\\ 5p+2\\cdot 10 & =0 \\\\ 5p+20 & =0 \\\\ 5p & =-20 \\\\ p & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $p=-4,\\,q=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0699480b-fcdd-5aa7-bd88-db69b130f0d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-16,\\,y=-63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561602} {:answer/id #uuid "6bef9576-7456-5c97-8c11-0c2e8958fa4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-12,\\,y=-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561851} {:answer/id #uuid "8db89ec1-822e-58c5-b2f7-ab0ed448f0e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=16,\\,y=32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561936} {:answer/id #uuid "b57c8f87-09f5-526e-925b-2fa685384d43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-32,\\,y=-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562033} {:answer/id #uuid "cbca4dac-d673-5f55-816e-2823843ec578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-63,\\,y=-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562091}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561602} :answer-field/id #uuid "61aac299-670f-5a9c-bbec-1956ec185e42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "be4c74c9-6e39-5f39-a1ce-fbc98923b17f" :question/math-academy-id "q-52203" :question/problem "Find the solution to the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{2}{7}y=2 \\\\ -x+\\frac{2}{9}y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $7$ and the second equation by $9,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{2}{7}y=2 & |\\times 7 \\\\ -x+\\frac{2}{9}y=2 & |\\times 9\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}7x-2y=14 \\\\ -9x+2y=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. We can eliminate $y$ by adding the two equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}7x-\\cancel{2y} & =14 \\\\ -9x+\\cancel{2y} & =18 \\\\ \\hline (7x-9x) & =(14+18) \\\\ -2x & =32 \\\\ x & =-16\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-16$ into the first equation to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-2y & =14 \\\\ 7(-16)-2y & =14 \\\\ -112-2y & =14 \\\\ -2y & =126 \\\\ y & =-63\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-16$ and $y=-63.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e086611-7bec-50f9-b80f-9f7d167a7576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561855} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca432f5-bc7b-5a9d-8960-0c52d5ad9bb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8,\\,y=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561933} {:answer/id #uuid "b01ccc0c-d6e0-5c8b-8e5f-f9b6e7de0c3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4,\\,y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562018} {:answer/id #uuid "b92976d9-53dd-5053-b478-c050ea85c203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-6,\\,y=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562052} {:answer/id #uuid "cd47a55d-6fb7-5018-956e-7585cfc1af31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3,\\,y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562052} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1e0f312-cd0e-5c8b-a953-be01ee2625b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c2c2c978-aae7-58d4-874a-c874d05efc9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-75861" :question/problem "Find the solution to the  following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}y=-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{1}{2}y=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $4$ and the second equation by $6,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}y=-1 & |\\times 4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{1}{2}y=-6 & |\\times 6\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=-4 \\\\ 2x+3y=-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-1,$ then we will get a $-2x$ term that can cancel with the $2x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=-4 & |\\,\\times (-1) \\\\ 2x+3y=-36 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+y=4 \\\\ 2x+3y=-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable, and allows us to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{-2x}+y & =4 \\\\ \\cancel{2x}+3y & =-36 \\\\ \\hline (y+3y) & =(4-36) \\\\ 4y & =-32 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{32}{4} \\\\ y & =-8\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=-8$ into the first equation to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =-4 \\\\ 2x-(-8) & =-4 \\\\ 2x+8 & =-4 \\\\ 2x & =-12 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{12}{2} \\\\ x & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-6,\\,y=-8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8bc467-4f82-5758-99ba-9799d92a2ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561618} {:answer/id #uuid "8669ee5b-2412-57d2-b0aa-06341f0d07e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -1 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561918} {:answer/id #uuid "b71c6ea4-3aa2-5b7e-8818-b3735f517002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562040} {:answer/id #uuid "eb73eef6-715d-508e-a66b-4d05b04b5bf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -1 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562173} {:answer/id #uuid "f4688075-facf-538c-bcae-94936c913af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -5 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562191}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561918} :answer-field/id #uuid "d10e5258-d2f4-58bf-af8d-2359214f5726" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d78d725a-0502-5fe2-897f-ae0775056dbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-135535" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2\\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5-\\text{i} \\\\ -2+5\\text{i} \\\\ -4+3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}4+4\\text{i} \\\\ 1-5\\text{i} \\\\ 4-6\\text{i} \\\\ 1-4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1-2\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-4-\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{array}]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -1 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d46c852-71aa-5405-ba00-2818a084e780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561624} {:answer/id #uuid "0fccf0f9-84b6-532e-8097-b8950bb64529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561629} {:answer/id #uuid "63e9fcea-73e4-51ca-86d6-f52ac7fa3260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561825} {:answer/id #uuid "a895dde9-5b19-55fa-9e29-994ae8fb5495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561999} {:answer/id #uuid "c4bd1201-33ef-5c84-9772-978f9951dc3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562075}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561624} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d254612-5df9-5924-b8e3-9685b0af8029" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562228} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "891a977f-3292-58d0-80ce-2eb4ed53c07c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1,2}=-0.5\\pm 0.5\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561923} {:answer/id #uuid "91168c21-a6a8-5be3-b9a9-24deae624ffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1,2}=0.5\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561943} {:answer/id #uuid "97e08a12-bf6d-5ce4-a37a-f1a84b29e4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561956} {:answer/id #uuid "dde47295-8e18-52dd-8657-7324c4373346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562133}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561923} :answer-field/id #uuid "36aad4f6-443d-53e2-8ee7-dee965880354" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562256} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c394391-ef27-554c-be55-0b847082cc73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561660} {:answer/id #uuid "9b7f866f-f763-5ebb-943b-db5b9e32bc99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561968} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6ecbc1-18f7-5593-99c8-4e1802ead268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an asymptotically stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562168}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562168} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ccf70ea-36cd-52da-a930-ff1480d7052b" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562262} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26c3a655-0dec-5f7e-95cd-2db00d0dd192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561686} {:answer/id #uuid "387b8f14-b652-5717-8133-89a4f722a3ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561723} {:answer/id #uuid "5388f336-ceb4-56ec-9471-62e471e77e31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561792} {:answer/id #uuid "69a8ea1e-5e95-555b-b649-0bee577c4e6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561846} {:answer/id #uuid "d15425e8-6e9d-5ec6-b00d-cf995325a194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562106}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561846} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f54b2e5-b5e2-51e5-8a3a-e2a8b88f4fac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562264} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cb3f71f-259a-5095-9344-dafd4146d0d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561732} {:answer/id #uuid "5ab8f1c7-258d-59d8-b26f-954a7abb013f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "spiral source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561805} {:answer/id #uuid "78bd40f1-542b-5a2c-b827-6627ef08ce29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561882} {:answer/id #uuid "966ab456-50f5-59fd-b3a7-2234c6e8542b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561954}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561954} :answer-field/id #uuid "536d2a25-1a52-5d05-808f-819be5037ebc" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562276} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d6f0c7-9b83-5a4a-a516-008f08f1d41e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561604} {:answer/id #uuid "214c754a-6a8e-5464-8d1b-543e1a8b44cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561673} {:answer/id #uuid "48ca3043-7c14-54ad-be51-58704e88bc94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561766} {:answer/id #uuid "c9aed290-0fa9-5512-9c02-8a43e48fac7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562084} {:answer/id #uuid "d52aa83e-9bf0-5b70-838b-ebb02d4dc2c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561766} :answer-field/id #uuid "762648f5-1207-5f3e-be39-97bd8eb92965" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562299} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ef6fa3-0c61-52da-9a58-b5f3581e26c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561681} {:answer/id #uuid "8c693a99-512e-5061-a753-dd7c3e3b934c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561932} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbe3370-6854-5660-a4dd-b09e0e41ba13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561940} {:answer/id #uuid "d65518ad-4812-5740-95e3-1539e00df576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562116} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c42440-43d6-5af8-9771-f53fa953e398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561940} :answer-field/id #uuid "95323910-156c-5adf-a3a0-32d0fac9f9e0" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e335f33f-c98e-5cab-bfc9-af13ddc0ea7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-334872" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-2y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0.5,\\,1]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-x)-xy)=2-4x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+4xy)=-2+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}2-4x-y & -x \\\\ 4y & -2+4x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2-4(0.5)-(1) & -(0.5) \\\\ 4(1) & -2+4(0.5)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -0.5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & -0.5 \\\\ 4 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-0.5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-0.5\\pm 0.5\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are complex with negative real parts, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f67813b-4012-57a6-aa60-6e1f4b03c49b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-4,\\,b=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561706} {:answer/id #uuid "4931d63f-6063-5b84-9f38-d5b26851222a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-2,\\,b=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561767} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac277a4-9539-5e8e-a05e-de4f499992b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-3,\\,b=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561929} {:answer/id #uuid "b09fc133-c399-5f21-8549-810bcfd05f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0,\\,b=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562020} {:answer/id #uuid "e66e2e44-a83a-5b53-8541-fd9e66215cf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=2,\\,b=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562148}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561706} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e76924e-7974-5bfd-a8ee-f608b55003f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ef9f41c7-171c-5068-8a8d-ff2c5135b2da" :question/math-academy-id "q-26475" :question/problem "Find the solution to the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2a+3b=-2 \\\\ \\frac{a}{3}-\\frac{b}{6}=-\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $6.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2a+3b=-2 \\\\ \\frac{a}{3}-\\frac{b}{6}=-\\frac{5}{3} & |\\,\\times 6\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2a+3b=-2 \\\\ 2a-b=-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Let's solve for $b$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a-b & =-10 \\\\ b & =2a+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $b=2a+10$ into the first equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+3b & =-2 \\\\ 2a+3(2a+10) & =-2 \\\\ 2a+6a+30 & =-2 \\\\ 8a & =-32 \\\\ a & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $b,$ we substitute $a=-4$ into the equation $b=2a+10$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =2a+10 \\\\  & =2(-4)+10 \\\\  & =-8+10 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-4,\\,b=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32bb690a-1edd-598a-8f92-be2849b7ba3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561715} {:answer/id #uuid "433b5384-8a47-5bf4-8248-feeb545bb5bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561745} {:answer/id #uuid "444d2ba3-d5e0-51cc-a7bf-2e59c0f7e046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561750} {:answer/id #uuid "c82b1b59-046b-5762-bdef-3f6575ddb33c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562080} {:answer/id #uuid "cda8ecc1-0eed-5b98-9c26-6689db877867" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562095}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561750} :answer-field/id #uuid "68f8092c-7c2b-5d58-ae67-c2f1ccabc279" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f2511236-69db-51c8-aebc-e7ad77137885" :question/math-academy-id "q-135531" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{array}{c|cc}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{array}],\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-7-\\lambda  & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )((-7-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-5(-4)) & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +41) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-10\\pm \\sqrt{(10)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 41}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "035ceaee-b6ca-5bb4-bfb9-11a4ca0e530b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561591} {:answer/id #uuid "2df44d7b-3fe5-59c3-a32d-b054a9cc4efb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561704} {:answer/id #uuid "58cb07a3-f5b3-5b99-8901-735dd9a5bbd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561800} {:answer/id #uuid "627e06b9-a4f1-547f-b90c-619445bcbf56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561822} {:answer/id #uuid "7480a3aa-70a0-5247-9a31-5c1c0daba1af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561871}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561704} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d98f8e9-9d6e-5d2c-b15c-295f14d86b6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fbaefedd-28e7-5c12-a8d5-d4c5ca3fba32" :question/math-academy-id "q-38885" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1+4\\text{i} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3+5\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{array}{c|cc}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{array}],\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the second and third columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563678 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05a3b4f0-37fc-510b-b782-70713c302e3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562949} {:answer/id #uuid "0e4b9832-4e75-5456-ae0f-c9cbef897a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562972} {:answer/id #uuid "79384773-3b33-5558-b1a4-01487217122d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563212} {:answer/id #uuid "d55768be-caa1-5015-a0a0-51da48688614" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563412} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b9c506-0e84-558b-99e5-7b80605f2397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563413}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562972} :answer-field/id #uuid "a41c8e29-8919-560f-acd3-bb2401f756f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "435bef4b-b3af-5768-973e-b4aa50ac129d" :question/math-academy-id "q-105235" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -9 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 10 & k\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 10 & k\\end{array}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-6\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-6.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-6\\,⇒\\,k=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ae222cd-009b-5eaa-a3a5-11a212b109ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563108} {:answer/id #uuid "4f925c03-7ab6-5520-8a1e-a5a9a717bba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563127} {:answer/id #uuid "5df4489a-0f0d-55e1-b4af-a317e094993e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563157} {:answer/id #uuid "8b7e78ed-ac31-5f29-8e01-0ec44695e0e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563259} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9e94e3-0927-55e7-9927-3ea8b44e2b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563108} :answer-field/id #uuid "a297791d-ebee-5b0c-89e6-af072a20920b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "648c2e1a-c875-5372-ad66-c0b63e6cb405" :question/math-academy-id "q-18760" :question/problem "Let $(a,b)$ be the solution of the following system. What is the value of $a+b?$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.05a-0.2b=0.04 \\\\ 0.12a-1.2b=0.24\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply both equations by $100,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.05a-0.2b=0.04 & |\\times 100 \\\\ 0.12a-1.2b=0.24 & |\\times 100\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5a-20b=4 \\\\ 12a-120b=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$ and divide the second equation by $3,$ we will get a $40b$ term in the first equation that can cancel with the $-40b$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5a-20b=4 & |\\,\\times (-2) \\\\ 12a-120b=24 & |\\,÷3\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-10a+40b=-8 \\\\ 4a-40b=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $b$-variable and allows us to solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}-10a+\\cancel{40b} & =-8 \\\\ 4a-\\cancel{40b} & =8 \\\\ \\hline (-10a+4a) & =(-8+8) \\\\ -6a & =0 \\\\ a & =0\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $a=0$ into the equation $5a-20b=4$ to solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a-20b & =4 \\\\ 5(0)-20b & =4 \\\\ -20b & =4 \\\\ b & =-\\frac{4}{20} \\\\ b & =-0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=0,\\,b=-0.2.$ So,\n\n$$\na+b=0+(-0.2)=-0.2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563702 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3e6f6d2-ce35-5cb0-a6c5-f9b0db8f35b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin(4(t-1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563409}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563409} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f7c85fb-7d21-5d48-8f24-4c37d6bc47ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563524} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8ac02a8-cb92-5cdb-baec-1e8c677c1a9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos(4(t-1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563502}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563502} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ff392b1-754b-5f5c-ad8a-af1f4c91dfa9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "701145a1-1f00-509b-9c1b-60626233c2a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-335434" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(1) \\\\ y(1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"130px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1))\\, \\\\ \\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1))\\,\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563719 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e4a4287-0b4e-5134-b26d-e720107fbfce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563012} {:answer/id #uuid "32966283-95f9-59c0-9da8-620246a44c9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563060} {:answer/id #uuid "35881d2a-16d1-5e15-a12f-b325e9f8db16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563066} {:answer/id #uuid "4d4a40a4-8887-524e-8b48-8a24128d12fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563117} {:answer/id #uuid "d639618d-a932-5b03-8225-fae8a0b21ef5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563414}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563066} :answer-field/id #uuid "283164aa-73de-5dcd-8a69-e6c9e6374abc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a8427e64-b547-5ecb-a62e-824aa188b632" :question/math-academy-id "q-34114" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -7\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -7\\end{array}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+(-1){a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot (-1)=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5afb7303-a034-559b-ab16-8b8311bec0af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v=6,$ $w=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563150} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd7e204-8744-5238-87a7-8727a3e0ad13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v=2,$ $w=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563152} {:answer/id #uuid "8355179e-84d2-53eb-b5bb-6abf063bd798" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v=3,$ $w=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563239} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b35f93-f5e9-58f7-a2a9-e55dc6e5f15f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v=-6,$ $w=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563319} {:answer/id #uuid "c6009515-0020-519f-8dc9-95fcc4128b0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v=-3,$ $w=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563381}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563319} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c21dd7c-6896-5689-b43e-0b6480bc4997" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b521b5c1-2f07-5bb3-9c22-0775852024a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-18691" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3.6v+21.6=0 \\\\ 3v+4w+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the top equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3.6v+21.6=0 & |\\,\\times 10 \\\\ 3v+4w+2=0 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}36v+216=0 \\\\ 3v+4w+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can easily solve for $v$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36v+216 & =0 \\\\ 36v & =-216 \\\\ v & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the value of $w,$ we can substitute $v=-6$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3v+4w+2 & =0 \\\\ 3(-6)+4w+2 & =0 \\\\ -18+4w+2 & =0 \\\\ 4w & =16 \\\\ w & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $v=-6,\\,w=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563732 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1442cd36-01ab-57b5-b752-7b7131e887f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562987} {:answer/id #uuid "1f1cbd99-3722-58a8-94e3-9b80df51c48b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563016} {:answer/id #uuid "38fb4394-142f-596a-acba-652af141f7ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563073} {:answer/id #uuid "976c158b-03de-5101-af86-c772a635e855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563285} {:answer/id #uuid "98c56786-6605-5401-b672-c63cb1cecd33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563288} :answer-field/id #uuid "7847d689-0e36-5ac4-b435-bd1cf0642bc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c391d9c3-0c76-5e6f-b0c3-d6b4ee2c37cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-105199" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 8\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=3\\cdot 2=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563749 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012982fc-c0c6-5360-983d-5c24ecfa5a09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "162" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562940} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef56e44-9e80-5352-bfb2-5d35c8c19329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563087} {:answer/id #uuid "65e52e96-8639-5303-a9f5-06fb2d4d391c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563179} {:answer/id #uuid "84afc469-3802-566a-b03f-b05bcd3fd37e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563242} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb4217f-2b11-5476-8d7f-3c0e1e75e42b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563361}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562940} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d94e257-45da-5352-bd7b-f0680253dc5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ddfb229d-f01d-542c-8a6c-f2e9a5996154" :question/math-academy-id "q-34639" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, ${a}_{3}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=3 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=18 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3}=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -8 & 18 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 9\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 9\\end{array}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}=6 \\\\ {x}_{3}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=3.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(3)=3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=9.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{a}_{1}+6{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ 9\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+6\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=9\\cdot 6\\cdot 3=162.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "246dbb57-80e4-56b7-8e34-27bb9114f64c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563026} {:answer/id #uuid "45cfe9fb-b684-53e4-ab59-2be2f93d9858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563103} {:answer/id #uuid "4bdeeefb-735e-5811-8754-67e744ec5c92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563112} {:answer/id #uuid "63a09f8e-7c0b-5249-a0c2-f6b28192c424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563174} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e28dd3-0453-550c-862e-c838b2f1aca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563408}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563174} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ea9a430-3071-50b8-b617-7ec74f7a4ddc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eaac4ca3-6936-5237-9e98-3d7aa72b5385" :question/math-academy-id "q-335025" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "235f0230-c6a2-58fc-bd59-7672f354b6da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge {x}^{2},\\,y\\ne 2x\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564021} {:answer/id #uuid "aae1a469-d68e-59c0-a688-35b023ed0b2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y={x}^{2},\\,y>2x\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564227} {:answer/id #uuid "aef90284-fc08-5b4f-9e81-9caa92dfe802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y<{x}^{2},\\,y\\ne 2x\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564239} {:answer/id #uuid "b015e9e9-ed69-58a1-bc47-3d84c2927025" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge {x}^{2},\\,y>2x\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564244} {:answer/id #uuid "c6cc232c-cc23-5849-87bd-c3d853e16f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy-$plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564278}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564021} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbec5fcc-3c3a-5ffd-b0f3-92a5d7992122" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0f0c6fb0-68b0-537c-ab8d-954592bf8ad5" :question/math-academy-id "q-158561" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}}}{2x-y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y-{x}^{2}\\ge 0 \\\\ 2x-y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge {x}^{2} \\\\ y\\ne 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge {x}^{2},\\,y\\ne 2x\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "388279d1-e34b-50a4-9ce3-cc8dace5482c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564064}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564064} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a013588-c06c-5f55-940c-cbbd3057b90b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564382} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cfa8656-14de-5766-be79-8ae7b5309f14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564189}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564189} :answer-field/id #uuid "f188d9c6-552e-5855-a6ab-7fbed03f69d1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "338f9068-6af3-5a99-af8d-48b1ac33890b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333610" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}-{t}^{2}+3t-2 \\\\ -4t+8\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,-\n$$\n{{field-2}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ 2t-3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ 2t-3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{0} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2(0)}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{0}^{2}-0+1 \\\\ 2(0)-3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+1 \\\\ {c}_{2}-3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1=2 \\\\ {c}_{2}-3=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=1.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ 2t-3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "649a9222-abb9-59ef-b622-a080e0e367ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564127} {:answer/id #uuid "ab63172a-74c5-5c18-a8ba-084568e718c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564230} {:answer/id #uuid "c51de2e9-7a9f-5185-b3d4-4330e8045809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564275} {:answer/id #uuid "d42cc278-6a57-5ca0-921a-9ae2d0e2e661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564304} {:answer/id #uuid "f957faed-af43-5b2d-b1cb-4057f11bdadd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564367}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564304} :answer-field/id #uuid "c80c438a-7645-590f-8f18-5d9ec09d771f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "451d76ac-89a7-51b9-831d-a0c0d1695961" :question/math-academy-id "q-105286" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=4x-6y-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-6y-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-6y-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4-2x & -6-6y\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4-2x=0 \\\\ -6-6y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n4-2x=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n-6-6y=0\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(2,-1)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-2x)=-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-6-6y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-2x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-6-6y)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(2,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(2,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,-1)=det({f}^{″}(2,-1))=(-2)\\cdot (-6)-{0}^{2}=12>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)=({f}^{″}(2,-1))_{11}=-2<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(2,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b93e02-911f-5448-ab37-cc2c74c1efa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563983} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa4ebb0-8d31-5131-bf0e-7bb725b939ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564035} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe615b5-1980-5ec3-8f2a-a28dfe3cf0c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564078} {:answer/id #uuid "466b980c-756d-5db4-8e7c-797a12ae9d1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564086} {:answer/id #uuid "e608ec51-2291-5759-a881-171cd19404a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563983} :answer-field/id #uuid "11e59e1d-92dd-58fa-bf22-f25988693e4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5873b1df-0474-5771-9ef7-dcd0b294d997" :question/math-academy-id "q-105317" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nIII. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solution." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+20{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-13{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 20 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -13 & 0\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -13 & 0\\end{array}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(0)+9(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "173c308d-bf5f-51b2-8d9d-eb671f780d5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564000} {:answer/id #uuid "8033029a-ae28-5ac2-9529-3fecf69cf229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564164} {:answer/id #uuid "a0cae614-d634-5235-9e3e-6c34fd454900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564212} {:answer/id #uuid "c50acf6f-63b2-5f2d-8d18-c231d138b0b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564274} {:answer/id #uuid "d1710c61-c367-5d54-addf-e16baa633170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564301}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564212} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad24458e-078b-5e2f-83d8-1060a660d14e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "74b82340-7a7b-5e95-8461-8f632c1a4fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-81042" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\n \nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ is an stationary point of $f(x,y).$  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=det({f}^{″}(0,1))={0}^{2}-1\\cdot 1-=-1<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,1)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c3c2367-c89a-5a63-909a-8ab545f01ec6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge 0,\\,x\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563987} {:answer/id #uuid "269efb88-268f-56aa-a516-6833ee078794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564025} {:answer/id #uuid "94603c8a-00da-5e0e-8705-b52b7dbbcde4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>0,\\,x\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564199} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a12713-af0d-576a-8ce3-2bf1f61336d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564308} {:answer/id #uuid "fa533f6d-4ad6-5d08-9dfd-22ac37f25112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,\\,x\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564199} :answer-field/id #uuid "e365395e-e193-5723-b80a-2293c4ae62a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7a819c67-d4ea-5946-94af-b50fc8d8ad96" :question/math-academy-id "q-158186" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡y}{{x}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y>0 \\\\ {x}^{3}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}y>0 \\\\ x\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>0,\\,x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93ad8973-8142-5ec3-9098-90c172b538cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564198}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564198} :answer-field/id #uuid "68ebb6bc-8ecd-5644-95d4-7ec3ae49104a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564407} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d3c4717-e0dc-535a-af6f-a87c7e67b231" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564043}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564043} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2f40649-92a8-5fb9-9467-241241b205e5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564450} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8b19b21-147d-5fdc-8f4d-a5e1d36c4409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564282}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564282} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecb59f7c-3318-5766-b4e8-a59390b05bce" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "83030669-b615-545c-b0dd-a292bd26151d" :question/math-academy-id "q-333014" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}-6{t}^{2}+\\frac{41}{3}t-8 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}t+5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$\\,\\,{t}^{2}+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n  </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n    .\n  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$-2$\n  </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-6{t}^{2}+\\frac{41}{3}t-8 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}t+5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-6{t}^{2}+\\frac{41}{3}t-8 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}t+5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(\\frac{5{a}_{1}-8{a}_{2}}{3}\\right){t}^{2}+\\left(\\frac{5{b}_{1}-8{b}_{2}}{3}\\right)t+\\left(\\frac{5{c}_{1}-8{c}_{2}}{3}\\right) \\\\ \\left(\\frac{-4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}}{3}\\right){t}^{2}+\\left(\\frac{-4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}}{3}\\right)t+\\left(\\frac{-4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}}{3}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-6{t}^{2}+\\frac{41}{3}t-8 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}t+5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(\\frac{5{a}_{1}-8{a}_{2}-18}{3}\\right){t}^{2}+\\left(\\frac{5{b}_{1}-8{b}_{2}+41}{3}\\right)t+\\left(\\frac{5{c}_{1}-8{c}_{2}-24}{3}\\right) \\\\ \\left(\\frac{-4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+9}{3}\\right){t}^{2}+\\left(\\frac{-4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}-13}{3}\\right)t+\\left(\\frac{-4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+15}{3}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coefficients of like powers of $t,$ we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{a}_{1}-8{a}_{2}-18}{3}=0 \\\\ \\frac{-4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+9}{3}=0 \\\\ \\frac{5{b}_{1}-8{b}_{2}+41}{3}=2{a}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{-4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}-13}{3}=2{a}_{2} \\\\ \\frac{5{c}_{1}-8{c}_{2}-24}{3}={b}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{-4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+15}{3}={b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first and second equations, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=2,\\,{a}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the second and third equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=-1,\\,{b}_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these last two values into the fourth and sixth equations, and solving for ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=1,\\,{c}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+3t-2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0248fac4-7021-5d3c-8fe6-630d74a3d1b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563972} {:answer/id #uuid "2d59668a-5b18-5b17-be7f-d90767135530" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564044} {:answer/id #uuid "a19b5317-af1f-54a5-aa91-38cce6394e14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564214} {:answer/id #uuid "de17d5bf-c3c6-5996-a382-6447bcc6e98a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564319} {:answer/id #uuid "e5988f07-d1dc-55e6-8850-b106bbc457fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564328}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564044} :answer-field/id #uuid "e84dcffa-6797-50d5-aaea-ce1046d2837d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "848dcb43-d675-54a9-ae47-a5c4f5dfb881" :question/math-academy-id "q-157254" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $3{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2} & =3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3e6ed38-eb22-5a68-b4e4-a818bbc05c8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564220} {:answer/id #uuid "b121cf1a-7dc7-5909-a04d-7262bf0ba8dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x-y>0,\\,x-y\\ne 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564246} {:answer/id #uuid "c7eee758-44ec-5173-b8d7-7b012b545dcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x-y>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564281} {:answer/id #uuid "d680f939-d4c5-5d9b-a170-a6f596fd8729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x-y\\ne 0,1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564307} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d25956-37d7-5565-be7f-161a70ab6752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x-y\\ge 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564341}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564246} :answer-field/id #uuid "421fe0aa-3a0a-5d77-af83-754300b22259" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8a09ec73-a8f5-5e32-98fd-165c3e60bda6" :question/math-academy-id "q-158183" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y>0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y>0 \\\\ x-y\\ne 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x-y>0,\\,x-y\\ne 1\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e9e21c3-28d7-5744-b751-8735eef80c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564048} {:answer/id #uuid "45ccbd17-a611-5e51-8f96-c134314902f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564085} {:answer/id #uuid "6c912a39-aa9c-5584-bd27-ebb5f9b76c23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564139} {:answer/id #uuid "8c06bd3e-d522-582e-8e6e-842930f9ae84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564186} {:answer/id #uuid "ae08b2e2-ed79-5464-95a9-3d40e4f6d24d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564186} :answer-field/id #uuid "99733e29-1d7e-5770-81f4-dc925a89eb32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e0061631-6269-58a0-a054-4e33a330ba6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-139798" :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ {x}_{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=9.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8(9) & =-9 \\\\ 9{x}_{1} & =-81 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97a52fdf-3469-584f-9753-ff054f6fb144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564204}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564204} :answer-field/id #uuid "6da43cad-9102-5837-b0fd-1a8a586b0473" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564409} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf902f7a-5b81-5b56-a8db-6e1126ebbd99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564262}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564262} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d8e6467-de2d-5e39-8c4a-83e68c137488" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e4dc2c43-c8eb-5bcf-a39b-abeb3bbb2764" :question/math-academy-id "q-333596" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,-\n$$\n{{field-2}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0\\cdot {e}^{2(0)} \\\\ {e}^{2(0)}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-0}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}+1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=3 \\\\ {c}_{2}+1=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we have ${c}_{1}=3.$ From the second equation, we get ${c}_{2}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564534 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d5fb42b-48b0-5faa-b43e-a2cc7b0c3a9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1<x<1,\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564045} {:answer/id #uuid "47325857-bade-5abe-940b-58d047f337df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564087} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6f7aeb-aa8b-5188-b690-7774d66d49cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564233} {:answer/id #uuid "be3d5d38-7205-5015-a8b8-93d91f6b0ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ne \\pm 1,\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564259} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a17952-6dd7-5e95-8f91-a446078ae611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1<x<1,\\,y>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564302}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564045} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1449eb3-c226-5410-a948-814f6bd35c51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e70a4727-f486-57b6-aa40-dbf0dba8fb16" :question/math-academy-id "q-158191" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2})}{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}>0 \\\\ y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-1<x<1 \\\\ y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1<x<1,\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77f754b6-3412-584d-ba89-67035e23606c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565119} {:answer/id #uuid "9749750d-293f-5863-817e-e6019efcfb73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565166} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d21a6a-0db9-5004-bce9-f0f22743dbc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565184} {:answer/id #uuid "ba52f496-ec86-590f-89ec-2e6d908c3e02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565220} {:answer/id #uuid "e63f7738-750c-5af7-b550-1409a521290c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565280}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565119} :answer-field/id #uuid "eef364c2-b743-5663-ae5b-bf40b13e0fd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2d45985d-1bef-55fa-a31c-73d51b4f78be" :question/math-academy-id "q-95029" :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=-1-t+2{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t-{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=3-3t.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\},$ and $$\n\\displaystyle [q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-1-t+2{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(t-{t}^{2}) & =3-3t \\\\ -{x}_{1}+(-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})t+(2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =3-3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{array}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations, we get ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=-6.$ \n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle [q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3+(-6)=-9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16f90a27-6313-5109-bbb8-4f7772524d5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564974} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd13daf-ca48-59e9-825a-a46dcf520048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565008} {:answer/id #uuid "9d32efd9-ca00-5319-9be6-643c6af3ff42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565179} {:answer/id #uuid "bd09020a-ccf6-5dd6-a0c1-117aa3597348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565226} {:answer/id #uuid "f157e119-b980-53fb-8f72-ef9064237542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565302}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564974} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2e25b77-025f-5093-8bf8-7b6467b74964" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2e84799f-e1a6-5cc6-b626-825322837015" :question/math-academy-id "q-106779" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{B}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $$\n\\displaystyle [A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 & {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=6 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{array}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=6 \\\\ {x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=4.$ From the first equation, we get  ${x}_{1}=6.$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle [A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6+4=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4beb3a66-909a-5284-8f19-4108f422c07d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565048} {:answer/id #uuid "b8494486-b6be-5318-969d-dd99f4d62997" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565215} {:answer/id #uuid "c322b0fe-2579-53a9-a017-e308cf16a5b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565234} {:answer/id #uuid "d7634f67-a8f6-59d4-a750-b899dde278d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565257} {:answer/id #uuid "e34854be-3584-5770-a376-eca2a97d5e2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565277} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae5852dc-829f-545a-9915-7bda8a3606ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "881394e4-d1e2-502e-8f1c-cafafb97686c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106782" :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2-t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=3-2{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=3t-4{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\},$ and $$\n\\displaystyle [q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2-t)+{x}_{2}(3-2{t}^{2}) & =3t-4{t}^{2} \\\\ (2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})-{x}_{1}t-2{x}_{2}{t}^{2} & =3t-4{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{array}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{array}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ From the  first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle [q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3+2=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25571277-f7a5-5289-a8ba-fe98639976b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,2,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564996} {:answer/id #uuid "5224e1dd-be47-5d3a-96e7-1006ac12e8af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565057} {:answer/id #uuid "57b32dc3-3595-5c57-8a49-20c39bedb11f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565073} {:answer/id #uuid "ba07e6b8-5e9f-5a76-add8-0070f7af1151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565219} {:answer/id #uuid "d5124caf-6b29-53bb-9a3b-83f8084a1569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565253}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565073} :answer-field/id #uuid "c93dfc36-0f20-5d6b-ba00-c7c282eff3ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a321d767-b173-540f-8a02-df4dc515c060" :question/math-academy-id "q-127094" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+2), & 0\\le x\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 3$ is $f(x)=\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+2).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{6}\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)=0\\,⟹\\,\\pi x=0+k\\pi \\,⟹\\,x=k,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer.\nAmong these critical points, only four lie within the interval $[0,3].$ These are $x=0,1,2,3,$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,3.$ So, these are critical points as well, but they are included above.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,1,2,3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (0))+2)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1))+2)=\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (2))+2)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(3) & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (3))+2)=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(0)=f(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $0$ and $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "107579c4-226c-56f4-a2e2-579b1beca778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564965} {:answer/id #uuid "722274c3-742b-5a48-b51d-ae59fbe68344" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565111} {:answer/id #uuid "991ed807-57db-5621-8684-6e0503cebcf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565169} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e1b4b5-e50a-5c14-9473-88dec5bbe338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565271} {:answer/id #uuid "fb160850-a523-56ec-9e9b-f5b031875117" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565322}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565322} :answer-field/id #uuid "358ca9bd-a430-5e36-accd-2c83f3de20f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "edf4a097-cde0-5ac1-86d1-cd429ba599d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95018" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{B}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 9 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $$\n\\displaystyle [A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 9 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} & 3{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{array}{cc|c}6 & 9 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{array}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}6 & 9 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}6 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{9}{2} & -3\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+9\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} & =8 \\\\ 6{x}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle [A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7500e34d-8118-54e7-bd9f-4a712248dfb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565860}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565860} :answer-field/id #uuid "759d25c6-2087-5621-820c-1d640ea38f01" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566152} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a435757-c811-58a0-a8a9-6e8c5f74e36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565692}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565692} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f97737c-ae6c-590f-a770-d33c927a54f7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566159} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55f70a89-bcfe-5aed-b870-1901d78733aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565814}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565814} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa6b1fc8-4a0e-5fee-a4ba-3951cd3851a0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ed0a30c-70d1-57fa-8ba9-ce180a14b5a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-168889" :question/problem "The critical points of $f$ are$\\,$$(\\pm $ \n{{field-1}}\n$,$ \n{{field-2}}\n$)$ and $(0,$ \n{{field-3}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x & \\,16{x}^{2}y+12y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x=0 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}y+12y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}8x({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2)=0 \\\\ 4y(4{x}^{2}+3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $4{x}^{2}+3\\ne 0,$ from the second equation of the system, we obtain that\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ 8x({x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ 8x({x}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(0,0).$\n\nNote that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these points, they are also stationary points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567014 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26d0891c-161c-5834-b9eb-6586cb0c9bc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\in [-3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566446} {:answer/id #uuid "51695b2b-00d2-5afd-9514-8c7464cb7b59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\in (1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566517} {:answer/id #uuid "82ae7df2-949d-5f30-8f35-50b577c6c509" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566612} {:answer/id #uuid "ed75b313-51b7-5ed1-ad30-3536875d859b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\in [-3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566844} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a0d75b-e243-5815-8927-4c35ba1889f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\in [-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566859}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566859} :answer-field/id #uuid "f016f7be-fb1c-5a42-bf74-e51c3d868c27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0e2e4815-0beb-5581-be50-52ebe618cd02" :question/math-academy-id "q-156127" :question/problem "What is the solution to the following system of inequalities?\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}n+2<5 \\\\ 1-2n\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the system, we need to find the values of $n$ that satisfy both $n+2<5$ and $1-2n\\le 3$ simultaneously.\n\n- First, we solve $n+2<5\\,:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n+2 & <5 \\\\ n+2-2 & <5-2 \\\\ n & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/4e/4e6e1c1a65abf5b7b88d39bb4d2606d64d5bc9ae526925e9f869eab0927194e3.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Then, we solve $1-2n\\le 3\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2n & \\le 3 \\\\ 1-2n-1 & \\le 3-1 \\\\ -2n & \\le 2 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-2n) & \\ge (-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2n & \\ge -2 \\\\ \\frac{2n}{2} & \\ge \\frac{-2}{2} \\\\ n & \\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/8e/8e96c91b84e3c69b2e8f58251dbe21294ef8b23ffa695c1b7fbe53ad4f22b2fc.png)\n\nTo find the values of $n$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](images/cd/cd1d2ad0e357eb3117e5b61351afeda01a348bfff5aec1a2d2940c77ecae120c.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThus, the final solution is $n\\in [-1,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567049 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bdd4b60-f14f-50d2-b847-4bdd16d267bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566419} {:answer/id #uuid "83b88f62-1d28-56e1-a841-c2ee99ad0cc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566613} {:answer/id #uuid "8667624e-7412-5e69-8c2b-4b7000347ae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566617} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a5e440-b9bd-59ee-bcd1-249e5c90c4b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566746} {:answer/id #uuid "da56ad38-3809-587e-b262-509456ca2d2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566802}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566802} :answer-field/id #uuid "10f602da-737a-5f8e-8b4a-d5c8710780bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "71946d02-db78-542e-8e6c-2648f4f0692f" :question/math-academy-id "q-127916" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}7x+2y=1 \\\\ y+x=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The lines $7x+2y=1$ and $y+x=9$ intersect at exactly one point\n\nII. The determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $0$\n\nIII. The coefficient matrix is not invertible" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567056 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34eb197b-149f-5d9a-afa8-a6ca93aa4d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566467} {:answer/id #uuid "5b69baa7-2e6b-5f82-b116-2ed224f3bf11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566539} {:answer/id #uuid "fa05bc39-6e1b-5bc3-af0f-0d804cd6f0ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566866} {:answer/id #uuid "fbcaa457-bfaa-5494-a9c2-a8d94c3a4bc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566867} {:answer/id #uuid "fc03833a-d3cf-53aa-9504-793095f13c92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566868}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566867} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2c59380-365d-5ba9-8299-b4172c65a692" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "80beacf9-8114-56a0-8f57-abbecbd075fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-73560" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y=0 \\\\ kx+2y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has a unique zero solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The lines $x+y=0$ and $kx+2y=0$ don't intersect\n\nII. $k=2$\n\nIII. The coefficient matrix is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $k=2$ then\n\n$$\ndet\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }k & 2\\end{bmatrix}=det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2=0,\n$$\n\nbut  according to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "916544ca-e729-52f9-99f7-464a605534c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566649}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566649} :answer-field/id #uuid "30c5013c-7ef7-5aa3-9cbc-e11a67222488" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566898} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad0c248c-2575-5291-8824-399e68cd193b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566707}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566707} :answer-field/id #uuid "53abe454-b2dd-5ad2-8fe1-b231d8cd91f2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566914} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3df23fd-8dd2-5adf-999f-c1d609ac0517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566751}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566751} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8fccfc7-d496-5162-8471-a2686b179c57" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b2b2b02a-ff73-57d9-825a-216db2bab95b" :question/math-academy-id "q-208784" :question/problem "What is the solution to the following system of inequalities?\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y-5\\le 2 \\\\ 2y+7>11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$y\\le $\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To solve the system, we need to find the values of $y$ that satisfy both $y-5\\le 2$ and $2y+7>11$ simultaneously.\n\n- First, we solve $y-5\\le 2\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & \\le 2 \\\\ y-5+5 & \\le 2+5 \\\\ y & \\le 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/b0/b0107bec7ccb2c56036de405a29870e8cfcc166cc6405f425fc3296ec8f1be2b.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Then, we solve $2y+7>11\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+7 & >11 \\\\ 2y+7-7 & >11-7 \\\\ 2y & >4 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{2} & >\\frac{4}{2} \\\\ y & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/59/59523e51144ef776295ec43032def1bc0192351deb6294fbb2d56acd87bb2cac.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $y$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](images/98/981477f5336b96998b61b7ab18647609cb33c12984c14049621ac15c698c25a5.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThus, the final solution is $2<y\\le 7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e674786-3773-5608-a6fc-fb98f442c244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p\\in (4,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572915} {:answer/id #uuid "6d46c113-ad6c-5e71-975b-24d9fa413748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p\\in (-5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572959} {:answer/id #uuid "a48122cf-62c6-51b8-baec-3d0e7d115657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p\\in (-4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573035} {:answer/id #uuid "bc69fc81-dde3-504b-9bdc-e9632904c364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573063} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c70cb9-1512-5f74-8095-ae65fd580c38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p\\in (5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573116}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573116} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4b7ff8f-af4b-55e7-a7df-45025547835b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "46ac0cd5-cca2-5b10-8e71-7bc6828cb573" :question/math-academy-id "q-156126" :question/problem "What is the solution to the following system of inequalities?\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}p-2>3 \\\\ 1-2p\\le -7\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the system, we need to find the values of $p$ that satisfy both $p-2>3$ and $1-2p\\le -7$ simultaneously.\n\n- First, we solve $p-2>3\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p-2 & >3 \\\\ p-2+2 & >3+2 \\\\ p & >5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/11/1189f49b694afcfef7f10a19b523ebbcce3bb8ab13b69e60ebfd5a6c342ff10e.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Then, we solve $1-2p\\le -7\\,:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2p & \\le -7 \\\\ 1-2p-1 & \\le -7-1 \\\\ -2p & \\le -8 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-2p) & \\ge (-1)\\cdot (-8) \\\\ 2p & \\ge 8 \\\\ \\frac{2p}{2} & \\ge \\frac{8}{2} \\\\ p & \\ge 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/f8/f8bf3ae94c8048dcf9060c985300f9a808ae73158eeeef6e0f01f3f922d33ea9.png)\n\nTo find the values of $p$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](images/1a/1a4cd0c86983a39354eef09ad60d44f3815c082f443a3eb12bd14f6232988f65.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThus, the final solution is $p\\in (5,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "379db8a6-59ef-51c4-b19e-9f394c7241c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579773} {:answer/id #uuid "53211947-6a5e-5550-a9fd-02fc512f9761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579819} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe00558-03e9-57f5-8707-e8c649333f14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579997} {:answer/id #uuid "cbfe57bd-7775-59f7-84d7-f37bbb194218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580020} {:answer/id #uuid "fa273f97-c79b-553b-898f-0bb7eda1d10c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580112}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579997} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1edf15c-bfc8-577c-90c1-714bc095dd6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0b554d49-4da1-5f8d-bcf8-1cbbdc9c035d" :question/math-academy-id "q-152920" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{8}{9{x}^{3}}, & 1<x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\cdot (1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{8}{9{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{8}{9}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{6}\\,{x}^{6}){|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{8}{9}\\,x\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{6}-0)+\\frac{8}{9}(2-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}+\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05621084-a21c-5648-a5e3-fa0747369323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-{x}^{2}-4x-3}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579682} {:answer/id #uuid "10bcdb1b-77d9-54de-bc5e-22c712726498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+8x+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579704} {:answer/id #uuid "8511a661-79eb-5c7d-937b-e694550ea9b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+4x-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579903} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb0df74-5a55-5912-9ccb-a52f705af551" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-{x}^{2}+8x-12}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580053} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e88fd8-d2a3-5cfd-b0c8-80e396779ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-{x}^{2}+4x-24}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580107}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580053} :answer-field/id #uuid "810114f3-e5f3-51c9-b110-b211a2d024c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0f02b938-0c55-59ec-87bd-31014fecd531" :question/math-academy-id "q-89679" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}(4-x),\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<2 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $2\\le x\\le 5,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<2 \\\\ {\\int }_{2}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1, & x>5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{x}\\frac{2}{3}(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={-\\frac{1}{3}t(t-8)|}_{2}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}x(x-8)-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+8x-12}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<2 \\\\ \\frac{-{x}^{2}+8x-12}{3}, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1, & x>5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "858b508c-5bcc-5811-9cee-f5f76b7bcb1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579905} {:answer/id #uuid "a1fcdf00-eec0-54d2-97aa-0bb47f79d333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579956} {:answer/id #uuid "ae598859-4f45-535b-8fa0-d01e2f088bf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579971} {:answer/id #uuid "b1cb6250-8b70-5180-b864-8f9d292eb6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579975} {:answer/id #uuid "d8cf60ff-34c8-5740-95f8-f7cede3454ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580047}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579971} :answer-field/id #uuid "d54337b7-aa08-5217-81f2-0b4ed73f662e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2457a6e9-742e-55e0-a3d8-ba18517222a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-152898" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & 0<x<1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}, & 1\\le x<3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\,{x}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+x\\,{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-0)+(3-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d68eac1-bac1-5b1f-ba70-16c4121d03a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579697} {:answer/id #uuid "17ed3f90-2edb-58d6-af92-cb8ed8bc52de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579720} {:answer/id #uuid "26f11a53-0e84-5ecc-b38a-47f3dd6b2e04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579743} {:answer/id #uuid "b250ffe1-7b62-5ee4-beed-2ee680e75cec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579977} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd53218-874c-5c1a-8708-4177b3ba77c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1-{e}^{-2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580024}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579720} :answer-field/id #uuid "cec9f88e-b3a0-5c2d-aacf-3313eacbeb21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "287af6bb-4c2e-50b3-ba4e-53f16eff69cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-89639" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2x},\\, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 0,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}2{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & =-{e}^{-2t}{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}+1 \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ 1-{e}^{-2x}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6056336-ae32-523f-8b1f-050bd6477254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{2(1-x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580006}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580006} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0d3395f-30d5-5bc7-b085-43bb8895760b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e0805cf-95c8-57b8-a293-b57f48436d69" :question/math-academy-id "q-210759" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2-2x},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 1,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}2{e}^{2-2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & =-{e}^{2-2t}{|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{2-2x}+1 \\\\  & =1-{e}^{2(1-x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1, \\\\ 1-{e}^{2(1-x)}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15356167-2a71-53e4-a6c9-cd2284880775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579713} {:answer/id #uuid "6148660f-2314-5a3e-a3af-7c758c6e1678" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{1-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579835} {:answer/id #uuid "c72bac0d-784e-5d90-a2bb-51bd3f889da5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{e}^{1-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580008} {:answer/id #uuid "c959c3ce-dc7d-594d-ab9c-b001e8aced09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580013} {:answer/id #uuid "e53ffdb1-27b6-5065-a380-9889763b5ad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{1+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580065}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579835} :answer-field/id #uuid "46c1e076-997b-5a75-b54a-9141dbe51be6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "75986acf-1dcc-5fbd-97f3-e157f2c8ddfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-89564" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-x},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 1,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}{e}^{1-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & =-{e}^{1-t}{|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-x}+1 \\\\  & =1-{e}^{1-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1, \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-x}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "101503d8-ac1e-519c-b643-3145ef500675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579702} {:answer/id #uuid "34532bb4-3b6e-51fa-b8f4-8f1df6ae53b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579768} {:answer/id #uuid "6d52c566-ed29-5bd3-b9c4-4764a5933e23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579857} {:answer/id #uuid "d064234d-5324-5917-96ae-62821d5c1cc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580026} {:answer/id #uuid "e7cfb4b2-5304-54e2-9284-c03e92de6812" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580074}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579857} :answer-field/id #uuid "f95b7983-f575-5576-8124-46c508261fb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "90429821-063a-5b87-91d0-cc785ad38d6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126175" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0, \\\\ {x}^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 1,\\, & x>1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(\\frac{1}{2}<X\\le 2).$" :question/worked-solution "We can perform this computation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{2}<X\\le 2) & =P(X\\le 2)-P(X\\le \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =F(2)-F\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580294 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1a02644-c630-5baf-98f8-30ffb5e21200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{x/4}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580061}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580061} :answer-field/id #uuid "c878b9c0-4767-50cb-820a-5733e95b5869" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b3eb04e4-eece-5838-97e3-15d082496d0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-210765" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-|x/4|}}{8},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}h(x),\\, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/4}}{2},\\, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/4}}{8}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/4}}{8}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/4}}{8}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/4}}{8}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t/4}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x/4}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/4}}{8}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{x/4}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/4}}{8}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-t/4}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x/4}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-t/4}}{8}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/4}}{8}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-x/4}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/4}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/4}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/4}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b7c3d9d-1fd1-5f70-9c47-4900803d0762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579782} {:answer/id #uuid "802569cf-5709-5b01-9259-aa1ad3a04d46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579894} {:answer/id #uuid "83ecf558-f769-5e23-97d9-98ef5f4b44b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579901} {:answer/id #uuid "d645ad93-6b98-56ef-a361-7e5bae5035f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580038} {:answer/id #uuid "ddbc43aa-7747-52a9-854b-70895eaf95e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579782} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a67f7f6-e90d-5245-90b7-ff29bde3d16e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d05897e8-f758-5e47-b57b-20d36a936195" :question/math-academy-id "q-89720" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}, & x\\ge 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(X\\ge 4).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that for continuous random variables, it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of the interval. So we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 4)=P(X>4).\n$$\n\nWe can perform this computation using the complement, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>4) & =1-P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =1-F(4) \\\\  & =1-(1-\\frac{{e}^{-4}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecb408a7-75ff-5d63-a35d-6f8dc609f3a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-e^{1-\\frac{x}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580085}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580085} :answer-field/id #uuid "acca0f89-97c7-509a-9d5e-8af7e65ac398" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d5ad580a-4dd1-5d88-83bd-cbc9bbb0ae68" :question/math-academy-id "q-210760" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{1-x/3},\\, & x\\ge 3 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<3 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 3,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<3 \\\\ {\\int }_{3}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{x}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{1-t/3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-t/3}{|}_{3}^{x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-x/3}+1 \\\\  & =1-{e}^{1-x/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<3, \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-x/3}, & x\\ge 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04e68475-9a29-5548-b204-ebe0398ccbb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579681} {:answer/id #uuid "175b4ed2-c056-5608-a697-68ed2a005496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579719} {:answer/id #uuid "bfafb65d-b72c-5fd1-a691-bf2e8504d2d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579996} {:answer/id #uuid "e539e188-f9bb-5a06-8da8-100771cf0ee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580064} {:answer/id #uuid "e608e405-956c-58bf-b7aa-5a6a9f235dfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580067}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579681} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a30346f-d508-5d25-9109-bb88aaebdefe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f7d5abe7-fa39-5089-9721-1b70b1a86d4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-126172" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{2}, & 0\\le x<2 \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(1\\le X\\le 3).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that for continuous random variables, it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of the interval. So we can perform the computation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le 3) & =P(1<X\\le 3) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 3)-P(X\\le 1) \\\\  & =F(3)-F(1) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580322 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12b2d4dc-a566-5d1c-95f6-d74f6074f75d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{43}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579709} {:answer/id #uuid "9eaba4d3-8644-5755-b8bc-9d9fc20360c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579945} {:answer/id #uuid "be2b5308-21b4-5190-9907-08e68d362c57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579994} {:answer/id #uuid "e73f1758-773b-5a29-870e-c53c21c68385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580071} {:answer/id #uuid "ff93a56c-4c48-5001-b83d-61ce30518a58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580122}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579709} :answer-field/id #uuid "a21ba782-afe1-5dd2-be47-4f2fdebb7a04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f9eb15f5-e2de-5a79-a97c-d1ff32d29564" :question/math-academy-id "q-152924" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}, & 0\\le x<1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\,x, & 1\\le x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{4}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the fourth raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{4}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{4}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\,{x}^{8}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{1}{12}\\,{x}^{6}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(1-0)+\\frac{1}{12}(64-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{21}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25fb603f-99e1-55bc-9588-77e136b79039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6056" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580450} {:answer/id #uuid "492a6a5d-3a6f-5f71-b86d-8254bca584d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5566" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580510} {:answer/id #uuid "5403b4f9-e3bd-50a9-94a2-ee5fe8970f03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0906" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580527} {:answer/id #uuid "6b537724-55d3-50f0-841c-0c2a4b421cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6650" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580568} {:answer/id #uuid "9661636a-57b9-51c0-bed2-cb7d7f17f0d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0656" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580640} :answer-field/id #uuid "4029d8d3-196b-5f66-b128-4c9c3eb421c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "052845df-31c1-5ac6-8fdb-db6eae2b480b" :question/math-academy-id "q-108283" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(8),$ calculate $P(X<3).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x≈6.30168\n$$\n\n*The probability density function ${χ}^{2}(k)$ is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ\\left(\\frac{k}{2}\\right)}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A chi-square random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(k)$ with $k$ degrees of freedom has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ\\left(\\frac{k}{2}\\right)}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8).$ So, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16Γ(4)}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecalling that $Γ(n)=(n-1)!$ for any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(4)=3!=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $P(X<3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1}{96}\\cdot 6.30168 \\\\  & ≈0.0656\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580909 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23bed5a7-946a-5761-b3c6-c169f961d99c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{4}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580446} {:answer/id #uuid "2c3f4fae-e3f0-5ab1-aeab-69c1e6b34bbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580458} {:answer/id #uuid "4d845e5f-48d1-56a8-a9a9-92289951f110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580518} {:answer/id #uuid "7895e320-c61b-57ba-84d3-b9a150bd1eea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580593} {:answer/id #uuid "ba7089bb-a038-5d2b-a34b-9bf3c599cdb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580686}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580518} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd737fdb-a1db-508c-9c67-7a25aa8d3a5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "51526ec7-ea58-5f68-9175-85663bcbb887" :question/math-academy-id "q-108284" :question/problem "Given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8),$ what is $P(X<3)$ expressed as an integral?\n\n*Hint: The probability density function ${χ}^{2}(k)$ is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ\\left(\\frac{k}{2}\\right)}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A chi-square random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(k)$ with $k$ degrees of freedom has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ\\left(\\frac{k}{2}\\right)}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8).$ So, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16Γ(4)}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecalling that $Γ(n)=(n-1)!$ for any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(4)=3!=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $P(X<3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54980c57-8d6e-54ce-afcf-f92faebaf38b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0389" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580528} {:answer/id #uuid "6a3743e5-6d0b-53aa-987b-d95480c8639f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.9393" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580565} {:answer/id #uuid "af01cbd5-6518-5f2a-a3d6-f10e4118cdb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.3689" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580676} {:answer/id #uuid "bac8021b-3e1c-5ea3-bfa4-7891ea89dbc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0889" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580687} {:answer/id #uuid "f35dde90-b67c-53f7-bf21-15cec35bcef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9389" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580773}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580773} :answer-field/id #uuid "46d3ab4e-f9cb-5529-ad5e-f05fc5c34187" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f47d7654-b908-545b-ade8-3e63f03d90b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-108278" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(4),$ calculate $P(X<9).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{9}x{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x≈3.75560\n$$\n\n*The probability density function ${χ}^{2}(k)$ is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ\\left(\\frac{k}{2}\\right)}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A chi-square random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(k)$ with $k$ degrees of freedom has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ\\left(\\frac{k}{2}\\right)}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(4).$ So, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4Γ(2)}x{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecalling that $Γ(n)=(n-1)!$ for any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(2)=1!=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $P(X<9)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<9) & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{9}x{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 3.75560 \\\\  & =0.9389\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db68153-71e4-53f2-b5a4-e679e239b458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{196}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581063} {:answer/id #uuid "33ca4d42-f211-5f5a-a904-08fe1bb8b9ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581109} {:answer/id #uuid "3a2398cc-13db-5b4d-aa48-fa2b5af29aba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581128} {:answer/id #uuid "56663b96-4b26-5926-8e50-3dc0cefa35e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581177} {:answer/id #uuid "90ddd770-dc0f-545f-b30c-4fd6c7938c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{68}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581280}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581128} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea381cac-6ebc-5b25-9bac-19b3cbcfbdd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "26ca37f6-adee-50b5-bfff-61ba9660bf70" :question/math-academy-id "q-90561" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{8}-\\frac{1}{4},\\, & 2\\le x\\le 6, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute $\\text{E}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}x\\cdot (\\frac{x}{8}-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{24}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8})|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{x}{8}-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{32}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12})|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{68}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{68}{3}-{\\left(\\frac{14}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e1ba380-1ed1-5658-8eca-fb53e6edc0cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581065} {:answer/id #uuid "20b2425e-5cb3-539f-aa03-9fddcdb76854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581068} {:answer/id #uuid "57846172-7ccc-5606-a4eb-76a43bc93be2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581178} {:answer/id #uuid "888e4beb-b73b-5ef4-975d-d3d656fe01af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581263} {:answer/id #uuid "fc12ad82-9f91-56a5-88eb-baadc1786972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{51}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581497}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581065} :answer-field/id #uuid "792ef969-2718-5f5b-951d-bf43cbc24888" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5bf58e62-fb33-5cad-aca4-6dac3bc7e21a" :question/math-academy-id "q-90557" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{5}{4},$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{{x}^{6}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{5}{3{x}^{3}}|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}-{\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217b031d-17e0-5178-927c-d2947c71f584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581069} {:answer/id #uuid "22e8cd9b-497e-52ee-a92e-45a048e71a9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581074} {:answer/id #uuid "84b4db40-7fd4-5295-8d44-f55e0a96fc2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581252} {:answer/id #uuid "9468d78a-a22f-59c0-8544-8dd91f65ce28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581291} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0f6b97-d522-5f22-9ec7-13b009b1f30c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581372}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581074} :answer-field/id #uuid "d50a3151-c108-5fbc-b0b4-11a77735dc99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7f458b28-b165-5394-a076-48aa8347358f" :question/math-academy-id "q-131260" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=1,$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-x, & 0<x<1 \\\\ \\frac{10(x-1)}{{x}^{6}},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot (1-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{10(x-1)}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10x-10}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}){|}_{0}^{1}+(-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{10}{3{x}^{3}}){|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}+0+5-\\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "352cd4fa-d886-55d2-bdb1-89058f24c395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581116} {:answer/id #uuid "6528a101-6796-5c43-857f-9f895a29d761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581202} {:answer/id #uuid "ae65e855-30f3-5b9e-ba89-1a9c683cb08d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581342} {:answer/id #uuid "c87a2573-4317-5af2-91e8-b7f7ba782b6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581395} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a902da-c2a6-5e49-a44d-112724a28544" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581449}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581342} :answer-field/id #uuid "55f17e15-9e56-5eee-aa0d-e6dbce1b4f99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a053b5b5-cfb1-5eaa-ade2-573c6fe57bdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-90566" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11}{6},$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{27}, & 0<x<3, \\\\ \\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}, & 3\\le x, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{27}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{27}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{81}|}_{0}^{3}+{\\left(-\\frac{9}{x}\\right)|}_{3}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{3}-{\\left(\\frac{11}{6}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{83}{36} \\\\  & ≈2.31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581719 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b4c07c8-f712-5930-bfeb-0ee97a9e4127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.02" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581212} {:answer/id #uuid "962cf013-64e9-5b24-9fdc-c7e067512868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581292} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f402d5-5a5a-56e0-a863-ae02138b8281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581321} {:answer/id #uuid "ac448e08-ccb0-5449-8728-2ee20562ab52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581338} {:answer/id #uuid "eb04968d-cd9d-5d15-8ae9-2fdeb2603c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581463}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581212} :answer-field/id #uuid "c19359cc-f0df-5410-8b43-eb2230ef3b77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a9b26e16-3764-5428-977d-85cbeba99ae5" :question/math-academy-id "q-131259" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]≈0.6931,$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+4}{24}, & -4<x<2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}, & 2\\le x<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{x+4}{24}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}{\\int }_{-4}^{2}({x}^{3}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}){|}_{-4}^{2}+x{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{18}+\\frac{8}{9}+2 \\\\  & =3.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & ≈3.5-(0.6931)^{2} \\\\  & ≈3.02.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a1479f-7099-576b-a109-9298d80b71b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581045} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab613fb-e376-5dd6-82b0-8b1c022750fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581209} {:answer/id #uuid "90af1f81-4516-5ca9-be60-41ae6a8e33fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581279} {:answer/id #uuid "9f002b2e-5e48-593c-b601-874beeedeb30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581312} {:answer/id #uuid "f440c68b-0129-5be4-a769-58c4965bfe37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581209} :answer-field/id #uuid "74ce1887-cf59-52dc-86f6-98bf74280a34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e1bf4042-6970-5916-a89a-e9a8db2c8bbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-131165" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=2,$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12(x-1)}{{x}^{5}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{12(x-1)}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{x}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =6-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86380ab4-aea7-5ecd-8522-30d622590edd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582047}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582047} :answer-field/id #uuid "b15a4bb9-fe76-5ea8-87b9-4d80a6538e55" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "428061f7-7e8e-53e2-b574-aa6198a6a095" :question/math-academy-id "q-302092" :question/problem "Find the limit at $x=1$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-1\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ 2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x=1 \\\\ 2-{x}^{2}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=2x-1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(2x-1) \\\\  & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=2-{x}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(2-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left- and right-sided limits are equal to $1,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582368 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ededad9-ad84-52bc-abc9-ec75f56a5e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582068}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582068} :answer-field/id #uuid "580ae876-f774-5f1c-bd10-20699bea1984" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4ec0e444-9705-59db-b39a-d24b9743b1f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-302097" :question/problem "Find the right-sided limit at $x=32$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 32 \\\\ \\sqrt[5]{x}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {32}^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the right, we have $x>32,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt[5]{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {32}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {32}^{+}}\\sqrt[5]{x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[5]{32} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b7c4795-8d0f-5b31-b444-b8c0eb69c6fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581911} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa5bab2-e355-5b9e-855c-bb5bfef18953" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581936} {:answer/id #uuid "56ed21e2-2909-5f46-b033-e965dc351450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581976} {:answer/id #uuid "74229e23-9be4-54f6-9fd1-0b478353e051" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582016} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d2fcd5-d117-5f1f-84e0-61a08766f123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582120}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582016} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc788828-4f85-5a34-b8ca-5c13c0894148" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "67761f4c-4109-5a7f-ba9f-81b0ee52929b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36698" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=0$ from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={e}^{x}+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}({e}^{x}+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{0}+1 \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe right-sided limit $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in (0,1).$ \n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582387 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f88e25f-bbe5-55c8-a67a-7a6eaca03287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582003} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c33853-ed17-50cd-9365-55cdff773212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582102} {:answer/id #uuid "baf290cb-bee8-57a2-8f68-84f632bad35b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582131} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2e168b-113e-5f39-bd83-a96775488ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582202} {:answer/id #uuid "feea6b1b-cd69-570a-abf4-a0f9fb53e3df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582225}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582225} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eaf31c9-2b0a-5983-9665-20143d77a00d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8de3c8e0-f48e-5191-a8ca-d1dc2bb45bdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-36972" :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x\\pi }{4}\\right)+3x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,-2<x\\le 1 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{x\\pi }{4}\\right)+kx\\, & \\text{ for }\\,1<x<4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ exists. What is the value of constant $k?$" :question/worked-solution "In order for $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ to exist, the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x\\pi }{4}\\right)+3x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x\\pi }{4}\\right)+3x) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1\\cdot \\pi }{4}\\right)+3(1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+3 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=4\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{x\\pi }{4}\\right)+kx.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(4\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{x\\pi }{4}\\right)+kx) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{1\\cdot \\pi }{4}\\right)+k(1) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+k \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+k \\\\  & =4+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ 4+k & =5 \\\\ k & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d1bf89-74d9-58fc-ba1f-0da7f0ba4ffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581898} {:answer/id #uuid "236e2bf2-5004-5ba4-a363-18d2915a0b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581904} {:answer/id #uuid "7632959a-8d88-5caf-a48f-d8f4a1094aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582021} {:answer/id #uuid "b11cbdba-dd2e-5207-92d0-06d9761a9de9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582115} {:answer/id #uuid "be18ad51-77db-59f7-8d4f-74d51b0630a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582135}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582135} :answer-field/id #uuid "34a831a1-71a4-5255-8f6e-cec5a3d24ef3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "98d20c59-41cb-5b9e-90bb-67319a17d2bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-129895" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curve $y=|x|-1$ and the line $y=2.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}xy{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}xy{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-1,2]$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}xy{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-1,3]$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}xy{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [0,3]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/09/09707ef0347a6b67fc39c5247efbe19d22ca69b713738b5e3361cca5f965b021.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}xy{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}xy{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c27a22e-a962-5c0b-ac8d-509a3f413b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581873} {:answer/id #uuid "3159f13e-3fef-5ae2-94dc-f101368b42af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581922} {:answer/id #uuid "3590421d-1eeb-588c-a38c-edc09e04ea74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581926} {:answer/id #uuid "5f9409db-447d-5a05-bf8e-68bf7bc3cba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581985} {:answer/id #uuid "fc1091f2-f17e-5704-8165-3e2b5d5e8a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582221}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581926} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dcb8bc3-1df2-51b0-a0ed-fa7129b876fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "998b8bc8-26b8-5a82-a7bf-365462577ff5" :question/math-academy-id "q-86862" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=-x+2.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy)\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-2,1]\\times [-1,2]$\n\nII. $R=[-3,2]\\times [-1,4]$\n\nIII. $R=[-3,1]\\times [2,4]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/10/10bcdedc511d069ecf790e06a1fbac49dc9bebd66638363f4e8dad5cbb82c2e9.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy)\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and III do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle II covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b9bc917-694b-5b26-a947-7e56ac5a85dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581933} {:answer/id #uuid "73ba2999-3a93-559c-81bc-31067c33ad06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582014} {:answer/id #uuid "9f69eba4-e276-5ed6-9fc1-1c1a5210c0da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582085} {:answer/id #uuid "b223d231-2ac9-59e2-be1f-0c0ef7066a74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582118} {:answer/id #uuid "bdaf8487-32eb-5fa8-b739-838528dda92f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582134}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581933} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee4833cc-fed9-5b98-bc1d-f4ee7a1af9be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9c5a76ab-4aff-5864-ae3d-053a84c4533e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80506" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}-1$ and $y=x+1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y+y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-1,2]\\times [-1,3]$\n\nII. $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$\n\nIII. $R=[-1,3]\\times [-1,2]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/e4/e48f172079d6edc0b29356cafa30c82fcae0afd008e469ea8e0ad9a3660d6fd0.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y+y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles II and III do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle I covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "407c491f-597a-540e-afe5-9804d6c32809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {(-4)}^{+}}f(x)=-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581937} {:answer/id #uuid "4e5fd911-3d75-5770-b418-4dc0ec2bc0c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {(-4)}^{+}}f(x)=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581961} {:answer/id #uuid "63a23393-e817-58c0-a832-81d8ce9e1743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {(-4)}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581991} {:answer/id #uuid "862e281d-312d-5f62-914e-6ce4317d46f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {(-4)}^{+}}f(x)=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582046} {:answer/id #uuid "eb12e284-590c-5a37-8e58-d67d3bacf884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {(-4)}^{+}}f(x)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582199}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581961} :answer-field/id #uuid "cff8eaee-0d6b-5fb3-a549-f95a888917bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a17f4a77-7f20-57c3-98c8-d205f2e21ffb" :question/math-academy-id "q-36723" :question/problem "Find the right-sided limit at $x=-4$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-16\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le -4 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the right, we have $x>-4,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {(-4)}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {(-4)}^{+}}{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={(-4)}^{2} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19e9011e-d30b-5e10-9291-d40265afac60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581899} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc1bde0-6485-563f-9185-3f9e0736b4e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581918} {:answer/id #uuid "39de9fe9-d7e6-5340-9599-4eb9dbd22d64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581930} {:answer/id #uuid "5c6cb386-3a85-5860-8f5d-fbf0b27a915f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581982} {:answer/id #uuid "d63f7256-d231-5a58-849b-a3b58e673816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582169}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581918} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a797a02-698f-5dcd-af14-e554533c2d7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a96711f0-2d2a-53ff-8366-452fba0f03a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-129787" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curves $y={x}^{2}-4$ and $y=-x+2.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [-4,5]$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-5,5]$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-4,4]\\times [-6,6]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/1f/1f03bc85a6cd6f803c87a8fedaf8518af8306a57ff530481810af91a4d93c697.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement III is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "484a3c56-83cb-569f-b29a-b976e8efb857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581948} {:answer/id #uuid "75c2deec-b195-55de-a0bb-ca48e72196b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)={e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582020} {:answer/id #uuid "c3028d77-f2e4-56ee-9bfb-3457f5721b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582145} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f45c30-ac71-5bf6-ac1b-979ce5e596fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582218} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5e4c53-4741-5c58-85d4-82d41f4fa527" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582222}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581948} :answer-field/id #uuid "98dbb6e6-c1ad-5475-a51a-a4a6621ce293" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b78d0954-5486-536b-bf33-39d65569ca17" :question/math-academy-id "q-36734" :question/problem "Find the limit at $x=0$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+1 & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ for }\\,x=0 \\\\ {e}^{-x}+1 & \\text{ for }\\,x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={e}^{x}+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}({e}^{x}+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{0}+1 \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={e}^{-x}+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({e}^{-x}+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{-0}+1 \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left- and right-sided limits are equal to $2,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582403 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "000a9a3d-96f3-559b-bda4-a5a961befa38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{(0,1)\\times (0,3)}x\\sqrt{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581852} {:answer/id #uuid "48da97ec-5a87-5ad7-8d2b-3999ddca1f3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{(0,1)\\times (0,3)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(0,1)\\times (3,5)}x\\sqrt{y}\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581950} {:answer/id #uuid "4a2aefe8-8a96-550a-a8c6-9a37a9535733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{(0,1)\\times (0,3)}(x\\sqrt{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581953} {:answer/id #uuid "7469d93b-2232-528f-a86a-41e132fc585e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{(0,1)\\times (0,3)}x\\sqrt{y}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(0,1)\\times (0,5)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582017} {:answer/id #uuid "a92adcba-a1d4-579a-8e9c-58761815e679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{(0,1)\\times (0,3)}x\\sqrt{y}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(0,1)\\times (3,5)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582105}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582105} :answer-field/id #uuid "01205ae7-dc8f-5176-9642-377c0ccc47f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d10ccc73-4795-5132-917b-9833b7789e44" :question/math-academy-id "q-130041" :question/problem "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\sqrt{y},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<3, \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,3<y<5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{(0,1)\\times (0,5)}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the rectangle $R$ can be represented as a union of two smaller rectangles, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =(0,1)\\times ((0,3)\\cup (3,5)) \\\\  & =((0,1)\\times (0,3))\\cup ((0,1)\\times (3,5)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{(0,1)\\times (0,3)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(0,1)\\times (3,5)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{(0,1)\\times (0,3)}x\\sqrt{y}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(0,1)\\times (3,5)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582413 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "557e205d-19aa-5f53-b4e1-09e47c331d7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581973} {:answer/id #uuid "7fbc5d37-d24b-5c42-bfe7-3c155b939dfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582040} {:answer/id #uuid "8cea4d6e-c773-5f1b-bc88-338e2f3bdcc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582064} {:answer/id #uuid "cba610d1-b63c-5d04-8fee-868f4f3a507a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582156} {:answer/id #uuid "dfe60f62-66ea-5d13-b051-c7f75ea27bb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582182}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582156} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb67238e-e2ed-5b4e-8d71-b279623fa0f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f50e74c6-919a-5f71-bca3-e3a7949efcdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-130043" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{y},\\, & \\text{if}\\,-1<x<2,\\,1<y<e, \\\\ xy,\\, & \\text{if}\\,2<x<4,\\,1<y<e,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we have $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2}$, where ${R}_{1}=(-1,2)\\times (1,e)$ and ${R}_{2}=(2,4)\\times (1,e).$ So, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A= & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}(3{x}^{2})\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}(x)(y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =({\\int }_{-1}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y)+({\\int }_{2}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{-1}^{2}\\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]_{1}^{e}+{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =(8+1)\\cdot (1-0)+(8-2)\\cdot (\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =9+3({e}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582416 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c2163a6-d9a9-5e49-ae34-0016701b225b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581998}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581998} :answer-field/id #uuid "332d18bb-fdb1-5607-a76f-60ae86476e65" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582249} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0b32ad4-7bdf-5da0-84e2-58fcd999a204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582142}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582142} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a3d4542-2723-5813-b9c6-7ab3206b09e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ff9dd161-7bf4-5368-bfad-b6750476ab0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-339916" :question/problem "Find the left-sided and the right-sided limits at $x=4$ for the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x<4 \\\\ {e}^{x-4}+1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<4,$ so we use the expression $g(x)=x-2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(x+2) \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>4,$ so we use the expression $g(x)={e}^{x-4}+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}(\\,{e}^{x-4}+1\\,) \\\\  & ={e}^{4-4}+1 \\\\  & ={e}^{0}+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)=6,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186583023 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "145414d3-4551-5bc9-ab74-c447219c7285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582519} {:answer/id #uuid "1a8ffc86-7d75-5084-a7d7-3986ac4ea83b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582527} {:answer/id #uuid "4afba6b1-ca84-5b31-b2ca-a18956c59e1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582609} {:answer/id #uuid "8884eb81-d756-57a9-986a-4474718857ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582714} {:answer/id #uuid "c93620fd-b6b3-57f4-b497-16be838a8c54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582814}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582519} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcb44186-a0bd-550a-bc8e-45dd6ade367a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ab85875-8659-5af1-a05b-3ec68f62450f" :question/math-academy-id "q-152934" :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}+1, & x\\ne -1 \\\\ 0, & x=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $g(x)?$\n\nI. $g(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)$ exists\n\nIII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=g(-1)$\n\nIV. $g(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n1\\. Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n2\\. Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n3\\. Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the second branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\ng(-1)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, $g(x)$ is defined at $x=-1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}(\\frac{x}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}(\\frac{x}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}g(x)=\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}g(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $g(x)$ at $x=-1$ exists:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, we have that $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\ne g(-1).$  $\\,\\,\\times$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since not all statements I-III are true, the function $g(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186583028 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57b2351c-4b42-5782-aeb4-ec0e407f1ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582628} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b1d317-3f93-5e70-95c1-84859a45519b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582771} {:answer/id #uuid "d40b0bee-8288-59b6-b48a-10f20d1bef1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582835} {:answer/id #uuid "ef70f882-0ef3-596d-84ba-c368b6023f95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582871} {:answer/id #uuid "f049f70c-0cc9-5cc5-ab85-7a878a16353b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582874}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582628} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd63c2e7-6767-5ec7-88f0-13b182019cfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3e22f275-7bc3-534b-b3ee-edf97c6bfb95" :question/math-academy-id "q-152911" :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}36-{x}^{3}, & x<3 \\\\ 9, & x=3 \\\\ {x}^{2}, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=3$\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)$\n\nIII. $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3)$\n\nIV. $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n1\\. Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n2\\. Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n3\\. Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the second branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(3)=9.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=3.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}(36-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =36-(3)^{3} \\\\  & =9 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =(3)^{2} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=3$ exists:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=9\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=9=f(3).$  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since all statements I-III are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07ca1e6c-a9db-594b-9016-8d2f4f95e494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584520} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6885e2-6c79-56ac-a707-f154c368c4c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584582} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e4803a-3193-5a61-ad59-41fa6d8162b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584760} {:answer/id #uuid "c76dfb14-ed87-5c28-aa7c-c3d97f8ae0d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584821} {:answer/id #uuid "e259caa1-7c25-584a-8413-f8b7a68494ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584865}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186584520} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f02793e-5a9a-51f8-ade8-5864438f715e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "17f7ba10-4976-547e-9411-f559e227b76c" :question/math-academy-id "q-87241" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}k{e}^{10x},\\, & x<0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{10x}$ on the interval $-\\infty <x<0,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{10x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{10x}\\,\\text{d}x &={[\\frac{k}{10}{e}^{10x}]}_{-\\infty }^{0}=\\frac{k}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{10}=1\\,⟹\\,k=10.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a9c0043-e651-5fb5-b526-8b2ece9ccdf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584690} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3ad99f-3f53-5db5-a5e1-a47c938ebf2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584720} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a09bad-26b1-51c6-9d0a-6d1b782f6a02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584758} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ae9e35-428b-50af-899d-f04c9726549b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584770} {:answer/id #uuid "d231275c-9862-52a3-92b8-4a75dc92b107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584834}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186584758} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2bbc92d-447c-5432-93d8-983cd65826b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "25a69f9d-b645-54bf-9cbb-f1e3ce6f1da8" :question/math-academy-id "q-160441" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2x},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\le 0$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\le 0,$ we have $f(x)=2{e}^{2x}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}2{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{0}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585060 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60926318-7d67-5432-9d46-0d93389d552d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584651} {:answer/id #uuid "71277fdb-8c98-5365-b0f5-9cac1eb9f910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584662} {:answer/id #uuid "79a544b4-b85a-5763-b5b4-bf511cf8012b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584685} {:answer/id #uuid "a02eb47b-fd3b-548a-9be4-72be2e27720c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584749} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c8a0f9-7d9f-5951-a705-287434776a2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584815}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186584662} :answer-field/id #uuid "43b222a4-745f-52a7-b644-3d778e54f3a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4aa5fb59-6718-5462-9925-b4e1ad0a5f64" :question/math-academy-id "q-160454" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\le 0$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\le 0,$ we have $f(x)={e}^{x+2}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x+2}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-0 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585082 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bfc71c5-c318-5100-92d6-a9e7486c44ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584575} {:answer/id #uuid "5144d45c-59c3-5e35-9995-0b6b096386a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584636} {:answer/id #uuid "7c1c4d2d-7da2-5b14-9bc9-8c47daa57710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584693} {:answer/id #uuid "db8d5c95-0d4c-5016-9f68-a2ad0181652f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584852} {:answer/id #uuid "df3e4c71-b937-5253-8fb5-5d0bc306bfa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186584859}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186584575} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5f99d1c-5ad7-5a81-becd-abbb46d6367e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186585012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "88b2d9de-60dc-5457-8401-6605d8f70130" :question/math-academy-id "q-160443" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x},\\, & x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\le 1$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\le 1,$ we have $f(x)={e}^{x}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{1} \\\\  & ={e}^{1}-0 \\\\  & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
